.TH spell "" "Command"
.SH NAME
spell \- find spelling errors
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBspell [\-a] [\-b] [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
spell -- find spelling errors
Usage:	spell [ -a ] [ -b ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-a	Use American variant of the dictionary (default)
	-b	Use British variant of the dictionary
.HE
.B spell
collects the words used in each
.IR file ,
or in the standard input if none is given, and prints on the standard
output those that it cannot find in its dictionary.
.PP
.B spell
is a shell procedure.
It extracts the words used in the input with
.BR "deroff \-w" ,
which also strips out formatting commands, sorts them into a unique,
case-folded list with
.BR "sort \-uf" ,
and passes the result to
.B /usr/lib/spell
together with one of the two compressed dictionaries kept in
.BR /usr/dict :
.B clista
(American spelling) or
.B clistb
(British spelling).
.B /usr/lib/spell
case-folds each input word before comparing it against the (also
case-folded) dictionary; any word it cannot find is written, in its
original case, to the standard output, and also appended to the
history file
.BR /usr/dict/spellhist .
.PP
Options:
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Check against the American-spelling dictionary,
.BR clista .
This is the default.
.IP \fB\-b\fR
Check against the British-spelling dictionary,
.BR clistb .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/dict/clista\fR	compressed American dictionary
\fB/usr/dict/clistb\fR	compressed British dictionary
\fB/usr/dict/spellhist\fR	history of words not found
\fB/usr/lib/spell\fR	dictionary-lookup program run by \fBspellcmd\fR
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B deroff
.SH NOTES
Because
.B spell
compares whole words against an exact dictionary rather than guessing
at spelling, it will flag any word that is not in the dictionary,
including many correctly spelled proper names and technical terms.
Words believed correct but missing from the dictionary should be
added to the local word list by the dictionary maintainer; see the
.B READ_ME
file that accompanies the
.B spell
sources for how the dictionaries are built and maintained.
